Title: Mars Jeems's Nightmare
Credit: Adapted from the tale by Charles W. Chesnutt (1899)
Order: 3
Production Code: 1x03

> FADE IN:

EXT. THE MINERAL SPRING - DUSK

AMB: swamp frogs, water seeping through stone

A shady hollow at the foot of the sand-hills. Moss on a rock; a wooden trough; a bucket on a chain. The water smells of sulphur and iron.

Uncle Julius McAdoo kneels at the spring and fills a gourd. He drinks, wipes his mouth, and looks up into the lens.

JULIUS (TO CAMERA)
Dey's a kin' er dream a man doan wake up fum 'cep'n' he change. Mos' folks has 'em in de night. Some folks has 'em in de daytime, wid dey eyes wide open, en dey calls it bizness.

He lowers the gourd.

JULIUS (CONT'D)
I knowed a man had one. It lasted a mont'. En w'en he woke up, he wuz a diffe'nt man.

MUSIC: STING - a single plucked banjo string, held

JULIUS (CONT'D)
I doan ax you ter b'lieve it. I des ax you ter set a minute.

SUPER: "THE CONJURE WOMAN"

EXT. THE VINEYARD - STABLE YARD - DAY

A boy of about seventeen leans on a pitchfork in the doorway of the barn, watching a hen cross the yard. He has been leaning so for some time. A bucket is overturned at his feet.

Tom shifts, yawns, and does not pick up the bucket.

John, on the porch, watches him through a window, then steps out, coat on, with the decided face of a man who has made a decision. Julius stands in the barn door behind Tom, hat in his hands.

JOHN
I'm sorry, Julius. I should have liked to oblige you by keeping him, but I can't stand Tom any longer. He is absolutely untrustworthy.

JULIUS
Yas, suh. I knows he ain' much account, en dey ain' much 'pen'ence ter be put on 'im. But I wuz hopin' dat you mought make some 'lowance fuh a' ign'ant young nigger, suh, en gib 'im one mo' chance.

JOHN
(firm)
No, Julius. I gave him more than a fair trial, and he simply won't do. It's impossible.

Julius nods slowly. Tom, behind him, scratches his head.

TOM
(hopefully)
Does dis mean I gits de day off?

JOHN
It means you get your wages and a good-bye.

Julius closes his eyes briefly. He does not argue.

EXT. SANDY ROAD - LATE AFTERNOON

The rockaway rolling gently down toward the swamp; two big earthenware jugs rattle in the back. John and Annie sit; Julius drives, silent.

ANNIE
(looking at John)
You are very hard on that boy.

JOHN
I'm hard on no one. I was imposed on once too often.

Annie says nothing. Her eyes go to Julius's back.

EXT. THE MINERAL SPRING - LATE AFTERNOON

A man is cleaning the spring with a shovel, waist-deep in the mud. He raises his head.

MAN CLEANING SPRING
Ten minutes, sir, and she'll be fit.

John and Annie sit in the rockaway under the shade. Somewhere a bird. They have caught the native infection of restfulness.

SFX: hooves, hard and fast, from around the bend

A man comes round the turn of the road in a buggy behind a high-spirited horse. The horse is rearing and plunging; the man is beating it furiously with a buggy-whip. He sees them, flushes a fiery red, lifts his hat with one hand, bows constrainedly, and the horse bolts by, panting, wild with fear.

JOHN
(low)
He looks as though he were ashamed of himself.

ANNIE
(indignant)
I'm sure he ought to be. I think there is no worse sin and no more disgraceful thing than cruelty.

JOHN
I quite agree.

JULIUS
A man w'at 'buses his hoss is gwine ter be ha'd on de folks w'at wuks fer 'im. Ef young Mistah McLean doan min', he'll hab a bad dream one er dese days, des lack 'is grandaddy had way back yander, long yeahs befo' de wah.

JOHN
(amused)
What was it about Mr. McLean's dream, Julius?

The man at the spring is still shoveling. They might as well put in the time.

JULIUS
Mars Jeems McLean wuz de grandaddy er dis yer gent'eman w'at is des gone by us beatin' his hoss. He had a big plantation en a heap er niggers...

MUSIC: BED IN - slow banjo, major key gone minor

SUPER: "BEFORE THE WAR."

EXT. McLEAN PLANTATION - DAY (BEFORE THE WAR)

A big plantation, well kept. A line of field hands moving down the cotton rows at a pace that looks, at first glance, like a fast walk.

Jeems McLean, hard-faced, mounted, rides along the line. He doesn't speak. He doesn't have to.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Mars Jeems wuz a ha'd man, en monst'us stric' wid his han's. His niggers wuz bleedzd ter slabe fum daylight ter da'k, w'iles yuther folks's did n' hafter wuk 'cep'n' fum sun ter sun.

In the quarter at night, a banjo starts up. A man lifts his head. In the big house, a window opens.

MARS JEEMS (O.S.)
Stop dat!

The banjo stops. Mid-chord.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Dey wa'n't 'lowed ter sing, ner dance, ner play de banjo w'en Mars Jeems wuz roun' de place. En he would n' let his han's git married. Said he wuz n' raisin' niggers, but wuz raisin' cotton.

EXT. McLEAN PLANTATION - QUARTERS - DAY

Solomon, strong, quiet, and a young woman field-hand work beside each other in the cotton. Their hands touch. A glance. They take it for granted that no one sees.

JULIUS (V.O.)
W'eneber any er de boys en gals 'ud 'mence ter git sweet on one ernudder, he 'd sell one er de yuther un 'em, er sen' 'em way down in Robeson County ter his yuther plantation, whar dey could n' nebber see one ernudder.

EXT. McSWAYNE PLANTATION - PORCH - DAY

Jeems, in his best coat, bows over Libbie McSwayne's hand, on a porch. She is cool, composed, smiling; she has heard things.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Dey wuz one time de niggers 'lowed Mars Jeems mought git bettah. He tuk a lackin' ter Mars Marrabo McSwayne's oldes' gal, Miss Libbie.

INT. McSWAYNE PLANTATION - PARLOR - DAY

Libbie, alone with her mother, is sewing. She does not look up.

LIBBIE
I 'clare I could n' trus' myse'f wid no sech a man. He mought git so useter 'busin' his niggers dat he 'd 'mence ter 'buse his wife atter he got useter habbin' her roun' de house.

She sets the needle in. Her mother does not reply.

EXT. McLEAN PLANTATION - FIELD - DAY

Jeems on his horse, alone, in the heat. All the courtesy that had come over his face has gone. He rides down the cotton rows, hard, with a rawhide.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Now Mars Jeems got wusser 'n he wuz befo' he sta'ted sweethea'tin'. All his bad feelin's 'ca'se Miss Libbie th'owed 'im ober he 'peared ter try ter wuk off on de po' niggers.

In the cotton, he stops before Solomon and the young woman. They have not moved apart in time. He looks at them for a long while. Then he strikes Solomon, twice, with his whip hand. The young woman is led away by two men. Solomon, on his knees in the rows, watches her go.

JULIUS (V.O.)
He gun Solomon fo'ty, en sont de gal down ter de Robeson County plantation, en tol' all de niggers ef he ketch 'em at any mo' sech foolishness, he wuz gwine ter skin 'em alibe en tan dey hides befo' dey ve'y eyes.

EXT. McLEAN PLANTATION - OVERSEER'S HOUSE - DAY

A poor white man in dirty braces, with big yellow snaggle teeth and a rawhide whip coiled on his belt, leans on a porch post. Nick Johnson grins at the field hands as they pass.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Mars Jeems's oberseah wuz a po' w'ite man name' Nick Johnson. De niggers called 'im Mars Johnson ter his face, but behin' his back dey useter call 'im Ole Nick, en de name suited 'im ter a T.

EXT. FREE SETTLEMENT - NIGHT

A peck of corn on the stoop. Solomon, in the dark. The cabin door is open and no light in it; a tall woman stands in the doorway with a gourd. Aun' Peggy.

SFX: a gourd rattles, a hornet drone, brief

AUN' PEGGY
Dat is a monst'us small peck.

SOLOMON
Yas'm.

AUN' PEGGY
Come back ter-morrer night wid anudder en I'll hab sump'n fer you.

EXT. FREE SETTLEMENT - NIGHT (NEXT NIGHT)

Solomon, with a second peck. Aun' Peggy puts into his hand a twist of paper with a powder.

AUN' PEGGY (CONT'D)
Dis yer stuff is monst'us pow'ful kin' er goopher. You take dis home, en gin it ter de cook, ef you kin trus' her, en tell her fer ter put it in yo' marster's soup de fus' cloudy day he hab okra soup fer dinnah. Min' you follers de d'rections.

SOLOMON
(scared)
It ain' gwineter p'isen 'im, is it?

AUN' PEGGY
Oh, no. It's gwine ter do 'im good. But he'll hab a monst'us bad dream fus'.

SOLOMON
How long?

AUN' PEGGY
A mont' fum now you come down heah en lemme know how de goopher is wukkin'. Fer I ain' done much er dis kin' er cunj'in' er late yeahs, en I has ter kinder keep track un it ter see dat it doan 'complish no mo' d'n I 'lows fer it ter do. En I has ter be kinder keerful 'bout cunj'in' w'ite folks. So be sho' en lemme know, w'ateber you do, des w'at is gwine on roun' de plantation.

She shuts the door. Solomon stands in the dark, holding the paper.

INT. McLEAN PLANTATION - DINING ROOM - DAY

Rain at the windows; a gray day. A cook, with a shaking hand, carries a tureen. Jeems sits alone at the long table. She ladles. He eats, a great deal, and pronounces it good.

JULIUS (V.O.)
It happen' dat de ve'y nex' day wuz a cloudy day, en so de cook made okra soup fer Mars Jeems's dinnah, en put de powder Solomon gun her inter de soup.

Jeems puts down the spoon. Something has crossed his face. He lays his hand against his forehead and holds it there.

EXT. McLEAN PLANTATION - BIG HOUSE - MORNING

Jeems, in a traveling coat, with Johnson at the horse's head.

MARS JEEMS
I's gwine 'way on some bizness, en den I's gwine ter my yuther plantation in Robeson. I doan 'spec' I 'll be back fer a mont' er so. I wants you ter run dis place fer all it's wuth. Dese yer niggers is gittin' monst'us triflin' en lazy. I wants de 'spenses cut 'way down en a heap mo' wuk done. I wants dis plantation ter make a reco'd dat'll show w'at kinder oberseah you is.

JOHNSON
Yas, suh.

He grins, and snaps his rawhide against his boot. In the quarter, the hands hear it from across the field.

JULIUS (V.O.)
De way he kinder grin' ter hisse'f, en show' his big yaller teef, en snap' de rawhide, made col' chills run up en down de backbone er dem niggers w'at heared Mars Jeems a-talkin'.

MUSIC: STING - low, wrong note

EXT. McLEAN PLANTATION - FIELD - DAY

Johnson on a stump, in the cotton, calling the day's tasks. They are bigger. The rations are measured out smaller. After the day's work the hands are sent to the barn to do an hour's more.

EXT. McLEAN PLANTATION - BIG HOUSE - DAY

A buggy comes up the drive. Dunkin McSwayne, hat on the back of his head, at the reins. Tied to the seat behind him, wrists bound, is a stranger: lean, in a torn white shirt, barefoot, with a look of puzzled fury on his face.

JULIUS (V.O.)
About th'ee er fo' days atter Mars Jeems went erway, young Mars Dunkin McSwayne rode up ter de big house wid a nigger settin' behin' 'im in de buggy, tied ter de seat.

JOHNSON
Mars Jeems ain' home.

MARS DUNKIN
I fotch dis nigger ober ter Mistah McLean ter pay a bet I made wid 'im las' week w'en we wuz playin' kya'ds te'gedder. I bet 'im a nigger man, en heah 's one. He wuz tuk up de yuther day fer a stray nigger, en he could n' gib no 'count er hisse'f, en so he wuz sol' at oction, en I bought 'im. He's kinder brash, but I knows yo' powers, Mistah Johnson.

Johnson looks the stranger up and down with a grin like a split melon.

JOHNSON
I reckon you kin trus' me, Mistah Dunkin, fer ter tame any nigger wuz eber bawn. De nigger doan lib w'at I can't take down in 'bout fo' days.

EXT. McLEAN PLANTATION - YARD - DAY

The stranger stands in the yard with his hands tied before him. A little ring of field hands looks on from a distance. Johnson walks all the way around him, slowly.

JOHNSON
W'at 's yo' name, Sambo?

STRANGER
My name ain' Sambo.

JOHNSON
Did I ax you w'at yo' name wa'n't? You wants ter be pa'tic'lar how you talks ter me. Now, w'at is yo' name, en whar did you come fum?

STRANGER
(a long beat, honest bewilderment)
I dunno my name. I doan 'member whar I come fum. My head is all kin' er mix' up.

JOHNSON
I reckon I'll ha' ter gib you sump'n fer ter cl'ar yo' head. At de same time, it'll l'arn you some manners, en atter dis mebbe you'll say "suh" w'en you speaks ter me.

He draws the rawhide and hits the stranger once across the shoulders.

The stranger turns and looks at him, not with fear but with astonished contempt, as at a stranger who has been rude at a dinner party.

When Johnson raises the whip again, the stranger makes for him with his bound hands and sends them both sprawling. Hands run to pull them apart. The stranger, on the ground, is roped, stripped to the waist, tied to a post; Johnson counts forty, with a dozen thrown in for good measure.

SFX: the whip; the stranger's voice, raw, "wild man" noise; the hands looking away

JULIUS (V.O.)
De nigger went on at a tarrable rate, des lack a wil' man, but co'se he wuz bleedzd ter take his med'cine, fer he wuz tied up en could n' he'p hisse'f.

INT. McLEAN PLANTATION - BARN - NIGHT

The stranger on the dirt floor, chained by the ankle, with nothing to eat. He lies on his back and stares at the rafters. He is whispering something. We can almost make it out. It sounds like a man trying to remember a name.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Mars Johnson lock' de noo nigger up in de ba'n, en did n' gib 'im nuffin ter eat fer a day er so, 'tel he got 'im kin'er quiet' down.

EXT. McLEAN PLANTATION - FIELD - DAY

The stranger, with a hoe he holds like a rake. Johnson's rawhide across his shoulders, then his legs. He does not know how to hoe. He is told, over and over. He is whipped again.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Dat po' nigger got mo' lashin's en cussin's en cuffin's dan any fo' yuthers on de plantation. He did n' mix' wid ner talk much ter de res' er de niggers, en could n' 'pear ter git it th'oo his min' dat he wuz a slabe en had ter wuk en min' de w'ite folks.

EXT. McLEAN PLANTATION - OVERSEER'S HOUSE - DAY

Johnson, sweating, leaning on the post. Mars Dunkin's buggy is in the drive again.

JOHNSON
I's feared I 'll git mad some time er 'nuther en kill 'im befo' I knows it. Dis nigger ain' got no manners en no spirit. I'd break his sperrit er break his neck, ef he wuz mine. But he wuz only sont on trial. Tek 'im back.

MARS DUNKIN
(sighing)
Well, I knows ef Ole Nick could n' git 'long wid 'im, nobody could.

EXT. PATESVILLE - STREET - DAY

Mars Dunkin hands the stranger, tied again, to a trader with a gang of "likely niggers" in a yard behind a warehouse. The trader checks his teeth.

JULIUS (V.O.)
He tuk de nigger ter town dat same day, en sol' 'im ter a trader w'at wuz gittin' up a gang er lackly niggers fer ter ship off on de steamboat ter Wim'l'ton en fum dere ter Noo Orleens.

EXT. McLEAN PLANTATION - FIELD - DAY

Solomon at the fence at the end of the row. The overseer is on the far side of the field. From the pines behind the fence, a stick shakes a branch. Aun' Peggy beckons.

AUN' PEGGY
W'y ain' you done come en 'po'ted ter me lack I tol' you?

SOLOMON
W'y, law! Aun' Peggy, dey ain' nuffin ter 'po't. Mars Jeems went away de day atter we gun 'im de goopher mixtry, en we ain' seed hide ner hair un 'im sence.

AUN' PEGGY
I doan keer nuffin 'bout yo' Mars Jeems now. W'at I wants ter know is w'at is be'n gwine on 'mongs' de niggers. Has you be'n gittin' 'long any better?

SOLOMON
No, Aun' Peggy, we be'n gittin' 'long wusser. Mars Johnson is stric'er 'n he eber wuz befo'. Long ez we had dat noo nigger heah, he kep' Mars Johnson busy pa't er de time. Now he 's gone erway.

AUN' PEGGY
(sharp, straightening)
W'at's gone wid de noo nigger?

SOLOMON
Ole Nick done sont 'im back ter Mars Dunkin, who sol' 'im ter a nigger-trader up in Patesville, w'at 's gwine ter ship 'im off wid a gang ter-morrer.

Aun' Peggy's face changes. She raises her stick.

AUN' PEGGY
W'y did n' you come en tell me 'bout dis noo nigger bein' sol' erway? Did n' you promus me, ef I 'd gib you dat goopher, you 'd come en 'po't ter me 'bout all w'at wuz gwine on? Now by not doin' it I's feared you gwine spile my cunj'in'. You come down ter my house ter-night en do w'at I tells you, er I'll put a spell on you dat 'll make yo' ha'r fall out so you'll be bal', en yo' eyes drap out so you can't see, en yo' teef fall out so you can't eat, en yo' years grow up so you can't heah.

SOLOMON
Yes'm.

AUN' PEGGY
Wen you is foolin' wid a cunjuh 'oman lack me, you got ter min' yo' P's en Q's er dey'll be trouble sho' 'nuff.

INT. AUN' PEGGY'S CABIN - NIGHT

A fire. Aun' Peggy sets a roasted sweet potato in Solomon's hand.

AUN' PEGGY
I done goophered it 'speshly fer dat noo nigger. You better not eat it yo'se'f er you'll wush you had n'. Slip off ter town, en fin' dat strange man, en gib 'im dis yer 'tater. He mus' eat it befo' mawnin', sho', ef he doan wanter be sol' erway ter Noo Orleens.

SOLOMON
S'posen de patteroles ketch me?

AUN' PEGGY
De patteroles ain' gwine tech you. Des hol' on a minute.

She sprinkles him from a bottle. She hands him a gourd of grease.

AUN' PEGGY (CONT'D)
Rub yo' feet wid dis, so you kin run fas'. Rub some on yo' eyes, so you kin see in de da'k.

She puts a hand on his chest.

AUN' PEGGY (CONT'D)
En ef you doan fin' dat nigger, _I 'm_ gwine git you, en you'll fin' me wusser 'n de patteroles.

EXT. THE ROAD TO PATESVILLE - NIGHT

Solomon, running, impossibly light. Two mounted patrollers ride past and do not see him. One turns his head, listening; sees nothing, and rides on.

EXT. THE TRADER'S YARD - NIGHT

A pen; a shed; a guard at the door. Solomon walks past the guard. The guard doesn't blink. Inside, the stranger lies in a corner, asleep, his wrists and ankles bruised.

Solomon holds the sweet potato under the sleeper's nose. The sleeping man's hand rises, takes it, puts it in his mouth, chews, and goes on sleeping.

MUSIC: STING - a single plucked banjo string

EXT. THE WOODS ABOVE THE McLEAN PLANTATION - DAY

A Sunday. Birdsong; Solomon at a rabbit-trap at the edge of the swamp, alone, still half-worried. A man is standing under a tree, barefoot, pale and thin, in a poor white man's clothes.

SOLOMON
Fer de Lawd's sake, Mars Jeems! Is dat you?

MARS JEEMS
Yas, Solomon. Dis is me, er w'at 's lef er me.

He leans against the tree like a man who has walked a long way.

SOLOMON
You er lookin' kinder po'ly, Mars Jeems. Is you be'n sick, suh?

MARS JEEMS
No, Solomon. I ain' be'n sick. But I's had a monst'us bad dream. Fac', a reg'lar, nach'ul nightmare.

A long moment. The two men look at each other. Neither says what he is thinking.

MARS JEEMS (CONT'D)
Tell me how things has be'n gwine on up ter de plantation sence I be'n gone.

Solomon tells him: crops, horses, cows, hogs. When he comes to the new man, Jeems goes still, and nods. "Uh huh. Uh huh."

MARS JEEMS (CONT'D)
Now, Solomon, I doan want you ter say a wo'd ter nobody 'bout meetin' me heah. But I wants you ter slip up ter de house en fetch me some clo's en some shoes. I fergot ter tell you dat a man rob' me back yander on de road en swap' clo's wid me widout axin' me whuther er no.

SOLOMON
Yas, suh.

MARS JEEMS
You neenter say nuffin 'bout dat, nuther. Keep yo' mouf shet, en I 'll gib you a dollah.

Solomon's eyes go very wide. A dollar. He backs away down the path, saying "Yas, suh" over his shoulder, and begins to run.

INT. McLEAN PLANTATION - STUDY - MORNING

Jeems at the desk, in a clean shirt and good boots, composed. Johnson stands in front of it with his hat in his hands.

MARS JEEMS
Tell me all 'bout dat noo nigger.

JOHNSON
(uncertain)
Yas, suh. Mistah Dunkin fotch 'im ober...

MARS JEEMS
I heared a little 'bout dat quare noo nigger las' night, en it wuz des too rediklus. Tell me all 'bout dat noo nigger.

JOHNSON
(warming to it)
I tied him up de fus' day en gun him fo'ty 'ca'se he would n' tell me 'is name.

Jeems laughs, a great helpless laugh.

MARS JEEMS
Ha, ha, ha! Dat is too funny fer any use. Tell me some mo'.

JOHNSON
Had ter starbe him befo' he'd take holt er a hoe.

MARS JEEMS
Dat wuz de beatinis' notion fer a nigger. Puttin' on airs, des lack he wuz a w'ite man! En I reckon you did n' do nuffin ter 'im?

JOHNSON
(grinning)
Oh, no, suh. I did n' do nuffin but take de hide off'n 'im.

Jeems laughs until tears roll down his face. His laughter has a thin edge on it that is not quite mirth. Johnson's grin falters a little.

MARS JEEMS
Oh, but you is a monst'us good oberseah. You is de bes' oberseah in dis county, Mistah Johnson. En dey ain' nebber be'n no nigger-breaker lack you roun' heah befo'. En you desarbes great credit fer sendin' dat nigger 'way befo' you sp'ilt 'im fer de market.

He stands.

MARS JEEMS (CONT'D)
Fac', you is sech a monst'us good oberseah dat I reckon I doan need you no mo'.

JOHNSON
(astonished)
Suh?

MARS JEEMS
Somehow er 'nuther I doan lack yo' looks sence I come back dis time. Fac', I's feared ef I 'd meet you alone in de woods some time, I mought wanter ha'm you.

He takes up a ledger and lets it fall on the desk.

MARS JEEMS (CONT'D)
But layin' dat aside, I be'n lookin' ober dese yer books er yo'n, en dere's some figgers w'at ain' des cl'ar ter me. I 'spec' befo' I settles wid you fer dis las' mont', you better come up heah ter-morrer.

He drops the ledger and walks out. Johnson stands there, rawhide on his belt, for a long moment.

EXT. THE McLEAN PLANTATION ROAD - NIGHT

Johnson, on a mule, riding out through the gate with a bundle on his back. He looks back once. Then he rides on, fast.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Mars Nick Johnson lef' dat naberhood 'twix' de nex' two suns, en nobody roun' dere nebber seed hide ner hair un 'im sence. En all de darkies t'ank de Lawd, en 'lowed it wuz a good riddance er bad rubbage.

EXT. McLEAN PLANTATION - QUARTERS - EVENING

Jeems, in the yard, with all the hands in a half-circle. He is thin and a little shaken, but straight. He tells them something. They don't believe it. He says it again.

JULIUS (V.O.)
He tole de han's dey neenter wuk on'y fum sun ter sun, en he cut dey tasks down so dey did n' nobody hab ter stan' ober 'em wid a rawhide er a hick'ry. En he 'lowed ef de niggers want ter hab a dance in de big ba'n any Sad'day night, dey mought hab it.

SFX: a fiddle starts; a banjo; the sound of feet

INT. McLEAN PLANTATION - BARN - NIGHT

Dancing: all the people from the quarter, a lantern at the end of every rafter.

The door opens. Solomon, dusty from the road, hands down from a buggy the young woman, in a bonnet, bundle on her arm. He has fetched her from Robeson County himself, with a pass and a note from Mars Jeems.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Solomon ax' 'im ef he would n' please sen' down ter de yuther plantation fer his junesey. Mars Jeems say su't'nly.

EXT. McSWAYNE PLANTATION - PORCH - DAY

Libbie on the porch with a letter in her hand. She reads it. Her mother, behind her, watches her face. Libbie turns, takes her bonnet from the hook.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Wen de niggers see how fine Mars Jeems gwine treat 'em, dey all tuk ter sweethea'tin' en juneseyin' en singin' en dancin', en eight er ten couples got married.

EXT. McLEAN PLANTATION - LAWN - DAY

A wedding. Mars Jeems, in black, beside Libbie in white. In the cotton fields behind, the pickers are singing. All the hands are in clean clothes, at the long tables. Solomon and his young wife lift their glasses.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Mars Jeems's own junesey, Miss Libbie, change' her min' en tuk 'im back ag'in, en 'fo' long dey had a fine weddin', en all de darkies had a big feas', en dey wuz fiddlin' en dancin' en funnin' en frolic'in' fum sundown 'tel mawnin'.

SFX: fiddle, banjo, clapping, laughter, fading

EXT. THE MINERAL SPRING - DUSK (PRESENT)

JOHN
(dry)
And they all lived happy ever after.

JULIUS
Yas, suh, dey did. Solomon useter say dat Aun' Peggy's goopher had turnt Mars Jeems ter a nigger, en dat dat noo ban' wuz Mars Jeems hisse'f. But co'se Solomon did n' das' ter let on 'bout w'at he 'spicioned, en ole Aun' Peggy would 'a' 'nied it ef she had be'n ax', fer she 'd 'a' got in trouble sho', ef it 'uz knowed she 'd be'n cunj'in' de w'ite folks.

He sets down the gourd and speaks directly to John, sententiously.

JULIUS (CONT'D)
Dis yer tale goes ter show dat w'ite folks w'at is so ha'd en stric', en doan make no 'lowance fer po' ign'ant niggers w'at ain' had no chanst ter l'arn, is li'ble ter hab bad dreams, ter say de leas'. En dat dem w'at is kin' en good ter po' people is sho' ter prosper en git 'long in de worl'.

The man at the spring steps up. He has finished.

MAN CLEANING SPRING
She's ready, sir.

Annie looks at her husband, with an extremely slight smile.

ANNIE
That is a very strange story, Uncle Julius. And Solomon's explanation is quite improbable.

JOHN
(wry)
Yes, Julius, that was powerful goopher. I'm glad you told us the moral of the story. It might have escaped us otherwise. By the way, did you make that up all by yourself?

Julius's face assumes an injured look, more of sorrow than anger.

JULIUS
No, suh. I heared dat tale befo' you er Mis' Annie dere wuz bawn, suh. My mammy tol' me dat tale w'en I wa'n't mo' d'n knee-high ter a hopper-grass.

He takes up the jugs. He fills them one at a time. He does not look at John. He does not look at Annie.

EXT. THE VINEYARD - BACK PIAZZA - EVENING

SUPER: "THE FOLLOWING EVENING."

John, with a cigar, on the porch steps. In the lane below, a boy carries a bucket of water to the barn.

John freezes. The cigar stays at his lip.

JOHN
(calling)
Annie! My dear, what is that rascal doing here? I thought I discharged him yesterday for good and all.

Annie comes out onto the piazza, drying her hands.

ANNIE
Oh, yes. I forgot to tell you. He was hanging round the place all the morning, and looking so down in the mouth that I told him that if he would try to do better, we would give him one more chance. He seems so grateful, and so really in earnest in his promises of amendment, that I'm sure you'll not regret taking him back.

John, for a long moment, does not speak. His cigar goes out. Down in the lane, Tom puts the bucket down in the barn door, and does not look up. From the shadows beside the barn, an old man in a coachman's coat holds his hat to his chest and smiles at nothing.

EXT. THE VINEYARD - DUSK

Julius on the log, in the last light, looking into the lens.

JULIUS (TO CAMERA)
A man's gotta be kind ter somebody, ef he's gwine ter dream good. I doan make de rules. I des tells 'em.

He looks at the vineyard, at the new house, at the porch where the cigar-end glows. He looks back at us.

JULIUS (CONT'D)
Nex' time I'll tell you 'bout a mule.

MUSIC: STING - a banjo, one note, bent

> FADE OUT.

THE END
